<div class="wrap">
  <header>
    <div class="eyebrow">UI proof &middot; lobu#2515 &larr; owletto#732</div>
    <h1>The &ldquo;When&rdquo; editor authors one trigger, subscribed to many events</h1>
    <p>The form used to let an author stack any number of triggers. Each one carried its
       own <code>execution</code>, <code>output</code> and <code>active_run</code>, so two
       triggers could disagree about how a single matching event should run &mdash; and the
       runtime settled it with an order-dependent tiebreak the editor could not even
       reorder.</p>
    <p>Both halves ship together. The form now authors one trigger, and that trigger
       subscribes to several connector events at once. Dropping the first without the
       second would have been a net loss of capability.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span><b>base</b> f57f6cb7</span>
      <span><b>head</b> 19e81c0c</span>
      <span><b>lobu head</b> 695f8666</span>
      <span><b>diff</b> 5 files, +510 &minus;90</span>
      <span><b>captured</b> localhost:5192 (base) &middot; :5193 (head)</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="eyebrow">Before &amp; after</div>
    <h2>The When section on a new Behavior</h2>
    <p class="lede">Same browser window, same page, same scroll position. Only the two
       editor components were swapped between shots; each was served by its own fresh
       Vite process, because HMR does not pick up a <code>git checkout</code>.</p>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure>
        <img src="{BEFORE}" alt="The When section before the change: an explainer paragraph about OR semantics, a Trigger card, and an Add another trigger button.">
        <figcaption><span class="tag tag--before">Before</span>A paragraph explaining OR
          semantics and which trigger wins a tie, then the Trigger card, then
          <strong>Add another trigger</strong>.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="{AFTER}" alt="The When section after the change: the heading leads straight into a single Trigger card, with no explainer paragraph and no add button.">
        <figcaption><span class="tag tag--after">After</span>The heading goes straight to
          the Trigger card. No precedence rule to read, no second trigger to
          configure.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="eyebrow">What the button produced</div>
    <h2>Two triggers, and the rule you had to know</h2>
    <p class="lede">Read the last sentence of the explainer:
       <em>&ldquo;If several match the same event, the first listed wins for run
       options.&rdquo;</em> Each card below carries its own
       <code>Each event &middot; Queue &middot; Save in Lobu</code>. When both matched one
       event, array position decided which set applied &mdash; and the editor offered no way
       to reorder, so getting it wrong meant deleting and re-adding.</p>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="wide" style="grid-column: 1 / -1;">
        <img src="{TWO}" alt="The old editor with Trigger 1 and Trigger 2, each with its own trigger options row.">
        <figcaption><span class="tag tag--before">Before</span>Trigger 1 and Trigger 2,
          each with an independent run-options row. Note that <em>Manual</em> also vanishes
          from both tab rows once a second trigger exists.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

